# ==============================================================================
# Which Rocky Linux runtime the DaVinci Resolve setup downloads
# ==============================================================================
# This is the ONE place the runtime container image is named. Every script that
# needs it reads this file, so moving to a new runtime is a one-line change here
# and nowhere else.
#
# It is a plain shell settings file: NAME=value, no spaces around the =. It is
# read with `.` by /usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-install, so nothing in it may
# run a command.
# ==============================================================================

# The image the setup pulls. It is built from resolve-runtime/ in this same
# repository, by .github/workflows/build-resolve-runtime.yml.
AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_IMAGE=ghcr.io/stoneharborent/aquarius-resolve-runtime

# WHICH TAG, AND WHY IT IS "9" AND NOT "latest"
#
# The tag is the Enterprise Linux major release, not a moving "latest". That is
# deliberate and it is the digest policy for this feature:
#
#   * `9` always points at the newest build of the Rocky 9 runtime. Rocky 9's
#     library versions do not change under you — that is what Enterprise Linux
#     IS — so a rebuild picks up security fixes and nothing else. Somebody who
#     ran the setup a year ago and runs it again today gets the same userland,
#     patched.
#
#   * `latest` would silently follow us to Rocky 10 one day, and Rocky 10 has a
#     modern GLib, which is the exact thing this container exists to avoid. A
#     moving tag would turn a considered decision into an accident.
#
# So moving everybody to a new Enterprise Linux is an edit to THIS LINE, shipped
# in an OS update, which is a thing somebody chose to do.
AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_TAG=9

# The container that gets created on the machine from that image. One name, so
# that `aq resolve status` and the app-menu entries can always find it.
AQ_RESOLVE_CONTAINER=aquarius-resolve

# ==============================================================================
# THE COPY THAT IS ALREADY ON THE COMPUTER (NVIDIA edition only)
# ==============================================================================
# Since 2026-09-16 the NVIDIA edition of AquariusOS carries the runtime with it,
# as a single file, so that setting Resolve up needs no download of ours at all.
# It is about 330 MB — the same bytes the registry would have sent — and it is
# put here by build_files/63-resolve-runtime-bake.sh.
#
# ⚠️ IT IS A SEED, NOT A PIN, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE POLICY.
# It is where a machine STARTS. It is not where a machine is stuck:
#
#   * The first login loads it into the person's own container store and builds
#     the container from it. No network, no waiting, no registry.
#   * `aq resolve update` still re-pulls the tag `9` from the registry, exactly
#     as it always did, and moves an online machine onto a newer build of the
#     same Enterprise Linux.
#   * installed.env still records the digest ACTUALLY IN USE on that machine,
#     whichever of the two it came from. `aq resolve status` prints it.
#
# So an offline machine works on day one, an online machine still gets Rocky's
# security rebuilds, and nothing about the reasoning above this line changes.
#
# The AMD/Intel edition does NOT carry this file. Resolve on Linux is supported
# on NVIDIA and nothing else, so putting a third of a gigabyte into an image for
# people who cannot use it would be a cost with no matching benefit. On those
# machines the setup downloads the runtime the way it always has.
AQ_RESOLVE_RUNTIME_ARCHIVE=/usr/share/aquarius/resolve/runtime.oci

# The digest the baked copy really is. This file is WRITTEN BY THE BUILD, next
# to this one, and only on the NVIDIA edition — it is a fact about one build,
# not a setting anybody chooses, which is why it is not a line up there.
AQ_RESOLVE_BAKED_ENV=/usr/share/aquarius/resolve/runtime-baked.env

# WHAT IS PINNED, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# The image is pulled by tag, and the exact digest it resolved to is written
# into ~/.local/share/aquarius/resolve/installed.env on the machine at install
# time. `aq resolve status` prints it. That is the record of what a given
# machine is actually running.
#
# We do NOT pin a digest here, and that is a considered trade rather than an
# oversight. A digest in the OS image would mean the Rocky security updates in
# a newer runtime build could only reach people through an operating-system
# update — for a container that exists to be a STABLE userland, being one dnf
# rebuild behind is the wrong risk to take. `aq resolve update` re-pulls the tag
# deliberately, and prints the digest it moved from and to.
